  "numbered_source_solutions": [
    {
      "ordinal": 1,
      "source_number": "1095",
      "actual_task": "First traveler starts A-to-B at4 km/h; second starts1 hour later at5 km/h; cyclist starts another1 hour later and overtakes the two travelers10 minutes apart. Determine cyclist speed, without a stated overtake order.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "cyclist speed"
      ],
      "independent_solution": "Use constant motion on the same directed route while both travelers remain in motion. Let s be hours after the cyclist departs, and let its speed be u km/h. At that start the first traveler is8 km ahead and the second5 km ahead. Their positions are4s+8 and5s+5; cyclist position is us. To overtake both moving travelers requires u>5. Catch times are s1=8/(u-4) for the first traveler and s2=5/(u-5) for the second. Thus s2-s1=(20-3u)/((u-5)(u-4)). The10-minute gap is|s2-s1|=1/6 hour. Since the denominator is positive for u>5, split by the numerator sign rather than presuming which traveler is overtaken first.",
      "complete_order_cases": [
        {
          "condition": "5<u<20/3: cyclist overtakes the first traveler before the second",
          "equation": "6*(20-3u)=(u-5)(u-4), hence u^2+9u-100=0.",
          "admissible_speed": "(sqrt(481)-9)/2 km/h",
          "rejected_root": "(-9-sqrt(481))/2<0 cannot overtake either forward-moving traveler.",
          "exact_timing_check": "Writing R=sqrt(481), s1=(R+17)/12 and s2=(R+19)/12. Their difference is1/6 hour, both are positive, and s1>3. The travelers themselves exchange order3 hours after the cyclist starts, so this later first-traveler-then-second-traveler overtake is physically consistent, not an extraneous branch."
        },
        {
          "condition": "u>20/3: cyclist overtakes the second traveler before the first",
          "equation": "6*(3u-20)=(u-5)(u-4), hence u^2-27u+140=(u-7)(u-20)=0.",
          "admissible_speeds": [
            "7 km/h",
            "20 km/h"
          ],
          "exact_timing_checks": [
            "u=7: s2=5/2 hours, s1=8/3 hours, gap1/6 hour.",
            "u=20: s2=1/3 hour, s1=1/2 hour, gap1/6 hour."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "complete_speed_set_in_the_same_route_constant_motion_model": [
        "(sqrt(481)-9)/2",
        "7",
        "20"
      ],
      "route_and_boundary_qualifications": [
        "The two roots7 and20 require the initially nearer, faster second traveler to be overtaken first. The slower admissible root requires the travelers' mutual overtake before the cyclist catches them. The source says one then the other and does not specify either order.",
        "At u=20/3 both catches occur at s=3 hours, so the gap is0, not10 minutes. u<=5 cannot catch the second traveler while it continues toward B.",
        "A-to-B length is not given. For strict overtakes before reaching B, the route must exceed the later catch position:10 km for u=20;56/3 km for u=7; (155+5*sqrt(481))/12 km for the slower root. If endpoint encounters rather than true passing were allowed, boundary equality would require separate stated behavior.",
        "All three branches are possible on a sufficiently long route. The missing route length cannot be used to select one speed or one overtake order.",
        "The result uses one constant cyclist speed and the stated constant traveler speeds, with starts separated by the two successive1-hour intervals. No stop at B, turnaround, or passing a stationary arrived traveler is invented.",
        "The singular wording speed does not establish uniqueness. The source permits three admissible speed values under its unspecified order."
      ],
      "source_status": "complete three-speed result under the stated motion qualification; original overtake order remains unspecified, not guessed"
    },
    {
      "ordinal": 2,
      "source_number": "1096",
      "actual_task": "In10000 m speed-skating training, the first skater is faster by1/3 m/s, completes each lap2 seconds faster, and after10 minutes leads by half a lap. Determine both speeds and both total training times.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "first skater speed",
        "second skater speed",
        "first skater10000 m time",
        "second skater10000 m time"
      ],
      "independent_solution": "Let first and second speeds be u,v>0 m/s, with u-v=1/3, and let the common lap length be ell>0 m. For simultaneous starts at the same track position with uniform speeds and exactly a half-lap cumulative lead after600 seconds, their traveled-distance difference is600*(u-v)=200 m=ell/2. Thus ell=400 m is derived, not imported from a track standard. The per-lap difference is ell/v-ell/u=2 seconds, so ell*(u-v)/(uv)=2 and uv=200/3. Set u=v+1/3:3v^2+v-200=0. Discriminant2401=49^2 gives v=(-1+49)/6=8 or v=(-1-49)/6=-25/3. Positive speed excludes the second root; hence v=8 and u=25/3 m/s.",
      "complete_speeds_and_times": {
        "first_speed": "25/3 m/s",
        "second_speed": "8 m/s",
        "first_total_time": "10000/(25/3)=1200 seconds=20 minutes",
        "second_total_time": "10000/8=1250 seconds=20 minutes50 seconds"
      },
      "sufficiency_checks": [
        "For the400 m lap, first time48 seconds and second50 seconds differ by2 seconds.",
        "After600 seconds the first has traveled5000 m, the second4800 m, difference200 m, exactly half the derived lap.",
        "Both have not yet completed10000 m at the10-minute observation.",
        "The discarded root would give second speed-25/3 and first-8 m/s, not physical skating speeds."
      ],
      "qualifications": [
        "Common lap length, same starting position and constant speeds are the conventional training model. Different starting offsets or changing speeds would make the rate/lead data insufficient for this derivation.",
        "The half-lap lead is taken as exactly the cumulative lead stated, not merely a cyclic phase offset that ignores prior lappings. No unmentioned whole-lap lead is inserted into the primary answer.",
        "If only a half-lap cyclic phase were specified, an additional unknown whole-lap count k>=0 would yield200=(k+1/2)*ell, ell=400/(2k+1), uv=200/[3(2k+1)], v=(-1+sqrt(1+2400/(2k+1)))/6 and u=v+1/3. Such a different observation model would not uniquely force the primary numbers. It is recorded as a model qualification, not claimed as the original explicit meaning.",
        "Meters/second are retained for speeds, seconds and minutes converted exactly;10000 m is the whole distance, not one lap."
      ],
      "source_status": "both speeds and full times determined under the same-start/exact-half-lap uniform model"
    },
    {
      "ordinal": 3,
      "source_number": "1097",
      "actual_task": "Cyclist and motorcyclist approach an intersection on perpendicular roads from8 km and15 km away, at1/3 km/min and1 km/min. Determine when their separation becomes5 km.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "elapsed time or times for separation5 km"
      ],
      "independent_solution": "While both approach, take signed coordinates along the perpendicular axes as x=8-t/3 and y=15-t, where t>=0 is minutes after the stated moment. If both continue straight on those same roads through the intersection, these coordinates remain valid after crossing. Separation squared is(8-t/3)^2+(15-t)^2. Set it to25 and multiply by9: (24-t)^2+9(15-t)^2=225, hence10t^2-318t+2376=0, or5t^2-159t+1188=0. Discriminant1521=39^2 gives t=(159-39)/10=12 or t=(159+39)/10=99/5 minutes.",
      "branch_checks": [
        {
          "time": "12 minutes",
          "positions": "Cyclist4 km before the intersection; motorcyclist3 km before it.",
          "distance_check": "4^2+3^2=25.",
          "route_status": "Both still approach the intersection, so this occurrence needs no post-intersection premise."
        },
        {
          "time": "99/5 minutes=19 minutes48 seconds",
          "positions": "Cyclist7/5 km before the intersection; motorcyclist24/5 km beyond it if continuing straight.",
          "distance_check": "(7/5)^2+(24/5)^2=(49+576)/25=25.",
          "route_status": "Motorcyclist crossed at15 minutes; cyclist does not cross until24 minutes. This later occurrence requires continued straight motion after the first crossing."
        }
      ],
      "conditional_complete_time_sets": {
        "both_continue_straight_at_the_stated_speeds": [
          "12",
          "99/5"
        ],
        "both_are_still_approaching_before_either_crosses": [
          "12"
        ],
        "first_occurrence": "12 minutes"
      },
      "qualifications_and_hold": [
        "The source says traveling toward the intersection but does not explicitly state whether they stop, turn, or continue straight after reaching it. Original post-intersection route intent remains UNKNOWN/HOLD for asserting a later occurrence or an unconditional complete future time set.",
        "The first occurrence12 minutes is supported entirely before either arrival at the intersection. The second positive algebraic root is not unconditionally approved or unconditionally rejected.",
        "A negative signed coordinate at the second root is a legitimate position beyond the intersection under straight continuation; it is not by itself a negative physical distance or an extraneous root.",
        "If both stop at the intersection, after15 minutes their separation is at most3 km, so there is no later5 km occurrence. Other turning behavior can give a different future separation law.",
        "The conventional planar-road model uses perpendicular straight routes, simultaneous observation time and constant stated speeds. No later turn or new route is inferred from an author answer."
      ],
      "source_status": "first occurrence12 certain within the stated approach; complete straight-continuation set12,99/5 conditional; original post-crossing behavior UNKNOWN/HOLD"
    },
    {
      "ordinal": 4,
      "source_number": "1098",
      "actual_task": "Two travelers start simultaneously from A and B toward each other. At meeting, the A-starter has traveled2 km more. After meeting they continue at same speeds; first reaches B in1 hour36 minutes and second A in2 hours30 minutes. Determine route length and both speeds.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "A-B distance",
        "first traveler speed",
        "second traveler speed"
      ],
      "unchanged_respective_speed_interpretation": {
        "independent_solution": "Interpret same speeds as each retaining their own pre-meeting speed. Let first and second pre-meeting path lengths be a,b>0, with a-b=2, and common meeting time tau>0. Then v1=a/tau, v2=b/tau. Remaining lengths are b and a, so b/v1=8/5 hours and a/v2=5/2 hours. Substitution gives b*tau/a=8/5 and a*tau/b=5/2; multiply to get tau^2=4, hence tau=2 rather than-2. Their ratio a/b=sqrt((5/2)/(8/5))=5/4. Together with a-b=2, this gives a=10,b=8 and route18 km. Speeds are v1=5,v2=4 km/h.",
        "complete_result": {
          "distance": "18 km",
          "first_speed": "5 km/h",
          "second_speed": "4 km/h"
        },
        "checks": [
          "After2 hours, first traveled10 km, second8 km, difference2.",
          "First remaining8 km at5 km/h takes8/5 hours=1 hour36 minutes.",
          "Second remaining10 km at4 km/h takes5/2 hours=2 hours30 minutes."
        ]
      },
      "common_equal_speed_after_meeting_interpretation": {
        "independent_solution": "If same speed means both use one common speed w after meeting, then remaining first length b=(8/5)w and second length a=(5/2)w. Their difference2 gives(5/2-8/5)w=(9/10)w=2, hence w=20/9 km/h, a=50/9 and b=32/9 km, total82/9 km. A pre-meeting duration tau>0 may be chosen freely with constant pre-meeting speeds v1=50/(9*tau), v2=32/(9*tau); those produce the given2 km difference at simultaneous meeting, then both switch to w. The original data do not fix those two pre-meeting speeds under this reading.",
        "conditional_result": {
          "distance": "82/9 km",
          "shared_post_meeting_speed": "20/9 km/h",
          "pre_meeting_speed_family": "v1=50/(9*tau), v2=32/(9*tau), tau>0 hours"
        },
        "checks": [
          "(32/9)/(20/9)=8/5 hours for the first traveler's remaining leg.",
          "(50/9)/(20/9)=5/2 hours for the second's remaining leg.",
          "50/9-32/9=2 km."
        ]
      },
      "qualifications_and_hold": [
        "The exact source phrase about continuing with same speed does not specify each one's own former speed versus a newly shared equal speed. These yield the different conditional results above. Original referent/meeting-after-behavior intent remains UNKNOWN/HOLD.",
        "If both were required to have the same constant speed before and after meeting, simultaneous starts would give equal meeting distances, contradicting the stated2 km difference. That is not a consistent way to force18 km.",
        "The uniquely determined18 km,5 km/h,4 km/h result is valid only under unchanged respective speeds. It is not represented as recovery of the ambiguous source wording.",
        "Both post-meeting times are durations from the meeting, not from initial departure, and convert to8/5 and5/2 hours exactly.",
        "The route is the same segment and both continue to opposite endpoints without stops. No delay, speed-change scheme or equal-speed premise is silently selected as original fact."
      ],
      "source_status": "complete unchanged-speed result and a consistent shared-after-meeting family recorded; original same-speed referent UNKNOWN/HOLD"
    },
    {
