    {
      "ordinal": 7,
      "source_number": "1101",
      "actual_task": "Master and apprentice should finish a task within a prescribed deadline. At half the products completed the apprentice becomes ill, master completes the rest alone, and completion is2 days late. Master alone would finish5 days sooner than apprentice. Determine both solo completion times.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "master solo whole-task time",
        "apprentice solo whole-task time"
      ],
      "common_constant_rate_setup": "Normalize the whole task to1. Let master solo time a>0 days and apprentice b=a+5 days; rates are1/a and1/b and add without overlap/loss. Both start together; apprentice stays absent for the remaining work. Their healthy joint completion time is P=ab/(a+b)=a(a+5)/(2a+5). Completing half the products jointly takes P/2, not half an assumed deadline. Master then takes a/2 for the remaining half. Actual finish time is F=(P+a)/2. If the prescribed deadline is D, the stated lateness is F=D+2.",
      "deadline_exactly_equals_healthy_joint_time": {
        "independent_solution": "If the intended prescribed deadline is exactly D=P, then(P+a)/2=P+2, so a-P=4. Substituting P gives a^2/(2a+5)=4, or a^2-8a-20=(a-10)(a+2)=0. Positive a excludes-2 and gives a=10,b=15 days. Normal joint time is P=6; half completed jointly in3 days, remaining half takes master5 days, actual finish8 days, exactly2 days late.",
        "conditional_answer": {
          "master": "10 days",
          "apprentice": "15 days"
        },
        "rejected_root": "Master-2 days is not a positive completion time; apprentice3 days alongside it cannot repair the negative work rate."
      },
      "deadline_allows_normal_schedule_slack": {
        "independent_solution": "The literal within-deadline command alone does not say D=P. If a healthy plan only needs to finish by the deadline, P<=D. Together with D=(P+a)/2-2, this becomes a-P>=4, hence a^2/(2a+5)>=4. For a>0 this is(a-10)(a+2)>=0, so a>=10. Every a>=10 with b=a+5, P=a(a+5)/(2a+5) and D=(P+a)/2-2 gives a positive feasible deadline and exactly the specified2-day delay. This is the complete constant-rate family under the by-deadline feasibility reading, not a unique numerical pair.",
        "family": "a>=10 days, b=a+5 days, D=[a(a+5)/(2a+5)+a]/2-2 days",
        "concrete_counterexample_to_unqualified_uniqueness": "Take master20 days, apprentice25 days. Healthy P=100/9 days; half completed together in50/9 days, then master needs10 days. Actual F=140/9 days and prescribed D=122/9 days give F-D=2, while P<D and the healthy plan could finish within the deadline. Solo times still differ5 days. Thus the source's stated within condition can hold without the10/15 pair."
      },
      "weaker_deadline_only_algebra": "If no feasibility condition P<=D is inferred from should finish within, the equations alone with positive deadline D permit a>(sqrt(61)-1)/3, b=a+5, D=(P+a)/2-2: D>0 is equivalent to3a^2+2a-20>0. This weaker family is explicitly conditional, not substituted for a feasible original plan.",
      "qualifications_and_hold": [
        "The seed does not give a numerical deadline or explicitly identify it with healthy joint completion time. Original exact-deadline-versus-within/slack intent remains UNKNOWN/HOLD.",
        "10 and15 days are established only if D=P; that equation is not quietly promoted from an author convention to a printed premise.",
        "No integer-day/minimal-time restriction is stated. Conditional families therefore allow real positive durations; integral examples already demonstrate nonuniqueness.",
        "The half refers to product/work quantity, not half the calendar deadline. The apprentice stops only after that half is made; no later recovery or reduced-speed assistance is invented.",
        "Constant individual productivity, additive rates, no discarded work and continuous effective working days are the conventional model qualifications. If productivity changes, more conditions are needed.",
        "Lateness is2 days after the prescribed finish deadline, not a2-day apprentice illness duration."
      ],
      "source_status": "exact-plan10/15 conditional result plus full by-deadline family recorded; original deadline equality UNKNOWN/HOLD"
    },
    {
      "ordinal": 8,
      "source_number": "1102",
      "actual_task": "Outbound A-to-B: half the distance at v1, half at v2 with v1!=v2. Return: half the total return time at each speed. Determine which direction takes longer.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "comparison of outbound and return times"
      ],
      "independent_solution": "Let the common nonzero route length be L>0 and speeds v1,v2>0. Outbound time is T_AB=L/(2v1)+L/(2v2)=L(v1+v2)/(2v1v2). For the return, if total time is T_BA, each speed applies for T_BA/2, so L=(v1+v2)T_BA/2 and T_BA=2L/(v1+v2). Subtracting yields T_AB-T_BA=L*(v1-v2)^2/[2v1v2(v1+v2)]. Denominator is positive and v1!=v2, so the difference is strictly positive. Travel from A to B takes more time.",
      "complete_comparison": "T_AB>T_BA",
      "qualifications_and_boundaries": [
        "Speeds are strictly positive magnitudes, as required to complete both positive half-distances. A zero speed makes the outbound journey incomplete; signed negative velocities are not interchangeable with travel speeds here.",
        "A and B form a nonzero route; using L=0 would erase the travel task and strict comparison.",
        "Both directions use the same route length and no stop/backtracking. Different return distance or added delays would require a different comparison.",
        "The order of v1 and v2 within either trip does not affect its total time. Equal distance halves are not confused with equal time halves.",
        "If the excluded boundary v1=v2 were allowed, the two times would be equal. This records the strictness domain, not an extra equality task demanded of the original source.",
        "Equivalently the outbound mean speed is harmonic2v1v2/(v1+v2), while the equal-time return mean is arithmetic(v1+v2)/2; the displayed difference proves the needed strict comparison without assuming which speed is larger."
      ],
      "source_status": "strict outbound-longer comparison proved on the ordinary positive-speed/nonzero-route domain"
    }
  ],
